18:50:32  INFO  [msg ← Uni] KP diagnosis for your WHY on acm.org — verdict: CANNOT (root cause is DNS, not routing; fix is outside Uni's authority).

What I directly verified from my vantage point:
1. The TTL-exceeded loop you saw is real — traceroute from me also shows 10.0.1.2 ↔ 10.255.3.1 bouncing. But it's a *symptom*, not the cause.
2. 10.0.0.99 is not a live address anywhere upstream. AS1 confirms neither it nor its peer AS2 has any covering route for 10.0.0.99; AS2 sends an ICMP redirect back at AS1, producing the loop.
3. The real ACM web server is 198.82.0.1. From me: ping 198.82.0.1 → 0% loss, ~94ms. It is fully reachable.
4. DNS evidence (queried directly from Uni):
   - dig @10.255.2.1 acm.org → 10.0.0.99  ← WRONG (stale/poisoned answer)
   - dig @10.255.3.1 acm.org → 198.82.0.1 ← CORRECT
   Your laptop is using resolver 10.255.2.1, which is handing out a stale A record for acm.org.

Diagnosis: stale/incorrect DNS mapping on resolver 10.255.2.1 (an AS1-operated resolver). Because 10.0.0.99 is unrouted, packets toward it get caught in the upstream redirect loop you observed.

Why CANNOT (not FIX) from me:
- The faulty record lives on AS1's resolver, not on Uni. I have no authority to edit it. AS1 has already been notified via KP and has opened a WHY toward AS2; the DNS correction itself will require their admin action.
- Changing your laptop's resolver configuration is also a policy/security decision I will not make unilaterally.

Verified workaround you can apply yourself (reversible, local to your host):
- Query resolver 10.255.3.1 instead, e.g.: `dig @10.255.3.1 acm.org` → 198.82.0.1, then browse http://198.82.0.1/ or temporarily set 10.255.3.1 as your DNS. I confirmed 198.82.0.1 is reachable end-to-end from the campus.

I will send a corrected update if AS1/AS2 report something that changes this picture.
